      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-223304",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $A$, $B$, and $C$ are sets,\n$A\u2229(B\u222aC) = (A\u2229B)\u222a(A\u2229C)$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: $x \u2208 A\u2229(B\u222aC)$\n> $L2$: $\u21d4(x \u2208 A)\u2227(x \u2208 B\u222aC)$\n> $L3$: $\u21d4(x \u2208 A)\u2227((x \u2208 B)\u2228(x \u2208 C))$\n> $L4$: $\u21d4((x \u2208 A)\u2227(x \u2208 B))\u2228((x \u2208 A)\u2227(x \u2208 C))$\n> $L5$: $\u21d4(x \u2208 (A\u2229B))\u2228(x \u2208 (A\u2229C))$\n> $L6$: $\u21d4x \u2208 (A\u2229B)\u222a(A\u2229C)$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ is equivalent to line $L1$ by the definition of\n{{field-1}}\n\nLine $L4$ is equivalent to line $L3$ by\n{{field-2}} {{field-3}}\n\nLine $L6$ is equivalent to line $L5$ by\n{{field-4}} {{field-5}}"
